#. type: Content of the linux-firmware-git entity
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:6
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/firmware/linux-firmware.git"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><title>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:9
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:13
#, xml-text
msgid "About Firmware"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><indexterm><primary>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:16
#, xml-text
msgid "/usr/lib/firmware"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:20
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"On some recent PCs it can be necessary, or desirable, to load firmware to "
"make them work at their best. The kernel or kernel drivers look for firmware"
" images in <filename>/lib/firmware</filename>.  But in LFS "
"<filename>/lib</filename> is a symlink to <filename>usr/lib</filename>, so "
"the directory containing the firmware files is actually <filename "
"class='directory'>/usr/lib/firmware</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:30
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Currently, most firmware can be found at a <userinput>git</userinput> "
"repository which can be viewed in the browser with the URL <ulink "
"url=\"&linux-firmware-git;/plain\"/>.  For convenience, the LFS Project has "
"created a mirror, updated daily, where these firmware files can be accessed "
"via <userinput>wget</userinput> or a web browser at <ulink url=\"&sources-"
"anduin-http;/linux-firmware/\"/>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:39
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"To get the firmware, point a browser to one of the above repositories and "
"then download the item(s) which you need.  If you want all these firmware "
"files (for example you are distributing the system onto multiple hardware "
"systems), either install <xref linkend=\"git\"/> and clone <ulink "
"url='&linux-firmware-git;'/>, or open this URL in a browser and download the"
" latest snapshot listed in the <literal>Tag</literal> table."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><important><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:50
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"<anchor id='linux-firmware-symlink' xreflabel='create the symlink and "
"download its target'/> Note that some required firmware files (and "
"directories) are actually symlinks to other files (or directories).  Those "
"symlinks are not stored in the Git repository, instead they are documented "
"in the <filename>WHENCE</filename> file.  Each line beginning with "
"<literal>Link:</literal> describes a symlink.  If you are downloading an "
"item you need and it's such a symlink (or in a directory which is such a "
"symlink), you need to create the symlink manually and download the target.  "
"On the other hand, if you want all the firmware files, use the "
"<command>./copy-firmware.sh /usr/lib/firmware</command> command which will "
"copy the firmware files and create all the symlinks in the "
"<filename>WHENCE</filename> file, instead of a plain copy command like "
"<command>cp -r * /usr/lib/firmware/</command>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:68
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"For some other firmware, particularly for Intel microcode and certain wifi "
"devices, the needed firmware is not available in the above repository.  Some"
" of this will be addressed below, but a search of the Internet for needed "
"firmware is sometimes necessary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:75
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Firmware files are conventionally referred to as blobs because you cannot "
"determine what they will do. Note that firmware is distributed under various"
" different licenses which do not permit disassembly or reverse-engineering."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:82
#, xml-text
msgid "Firmware for PCs falls into four categories:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:88
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Updates to the CPU to work around errata, usually referred to as microcode."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:94
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Firmware for video controllers. On x86 machines this is required for ATI "
"devices (Radeon and AMDGPU chips) and may be useful for Intel (Skylake and "
"later) and NVIDIA (Kepler and later) GPUs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:100
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"ATI Radeon and AMDGPU devices all require firmware to be able to use KMS "
"(kernel modesetting - the preferred option) as well as for Xorg."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:105
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Intel integrated GPUs from Skylake onwards can use firmware for GuC (the "
"Graphics microcontroller), and also for the HuC (HEVC/H265 microcontroller "
"which offloads to the GPU) and the DMC (Display Microcontroller) to provide "
"additional low-power states. The GuC and HuC have had a chequered history in"
" the kernel and updated firmware may be disabled by default, depending on "
"your kernel version. Further details may be found at <ulink "
"url=\"https://01.org/linuxgraphics/downloads/firmware/\">01.org</ulink> and "
"<ulink url=\"https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/intel_graphics\">Arch "
"linux</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:119
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"NVIDIA GPUs from Kepler onwards require signed firmware, otherwise the "
"nouveau driver is unable to provide hardware acceleration. NVIDIA has now "
"released firmware up to Ada Lovelace (GeForce 40 series) to linux-firmware."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:127
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Firmware updates for wired network ports. Most of them work even without the"
" updates, but they will probably work better with the updated firmware. For "
"some modern laptops, firmware for both wired ethernet (e.g. rtl_nic) and "
"also for bluetooth devices (e.g. qca)  is <emphasis>required</emphasis> "
"before the wired network can be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><itemizedlist><listitem><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:136
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Firmware for other devices, such as wireless NICs. These devices are not "
"required for the PC to boot, but need the firmware before these devices can "
"be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><note><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:145
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Although not needed to load a firmware blob, the following tools may be "
"useful for determining, obtaining, or preparing the needed firmware in order"
" to load it into the system: <xref linkend=\"cpio\"/>, <xref "
"linkend=\"git\"/>, <xref linkend=\"pciutils\"/>, and <xref "
"linkend=\"wget\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><title>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:157
#, xml-text
msgid "Microcode updates for CPUs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:160
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"In general, microcode can be loaded by the BIOS or UEFI, and it might be "
"updated by upgrading to a newer version of those. On linux, you can also "
"load the microcode from the kernel if you are using an AMD family 10h or "
"later processor (first introduced late 2007), or an Intel processor from "
"1998 and later (Pentium4, Core, etc), if updated microcode has been "
"released. These updates only last until the machine is powered off, so they "
"need to be applied on every boot."
msgstr ""

#.  deliberate hard coded version, not a bug
#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><note><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:171
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"In some rare cases a microcode update from the kernel can be ineffective.  "
"For example, despite intel-microcode-20241029 contains the Raptor Lake "
"microcode revision 0x12b which is intended to fix a notorious excessive "
"voltage issue causing stability issues and even permanent CPU damages, when "
"the kernel boots it's already <ulink url='https://github.com/intel/Intel-"
"Linux-Processor-Microcode-Data-Files/issues/78#issuecomment-2286857023'>too "
"late</ulink> for the microcode to fix the issue.  The only way to fix this "
"issue is updating the BIOS."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:184
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Intel provide updates of their microcode for Skylake and later processors as"
" new vulnerabilities come to light, and have in the past provided updates "
"for processors from SandyBridge onwards, although those are no-longer "
"supported for new fixes. New versions of AMD firmware are rare and usually "
"only apply to a few models, although motherboard manufacturers get AGESA "
"(AMD Generic Encapsulated Software Architecture) updates to change BIOS "
"values, e.g. to support more memory variants, new vulnerability fixes or "
"newer CPUs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:195
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"There were two ways of loading the microcode, described as 'early' and "
"'late'. Early loading happens before userspace has been started, late "
"loading happens after userspace has started. However, late loading is known "
"to be problematic and not supported anymore (see the kernel commit <ulink "
"url=\"https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=d23d33e\">"
" x86/microcode: Taint and warn on late loading</ulink>).  Indeed, early "
"loading is needed to work around one particular erratum in early Intel "
"Haswell processors which had TSX enabled.  (See <ulink url= "
"\"https://www.anandtech.com/show/8376/intel-disables-tsx-instructions-"
"erratum-found-in-haswell-haswelleep-broadwelly/\"> Intel Disables TSX "
"Instructions: Erratum Found in Haswell, Haswell-E/EP, Broadwell-Y</ulink>.)"
"  Without this update glibc can do the wrong thing in uncommon situations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:211
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"In previous versions of this book, late loading of microcode to see if it "
"gets applied was recommended, followed by using an initrd to force early "
"loading. But now that the contents of the Intel microcode tarball is "
"documented, and AMD microcode can be read by a Python script to determine "
"which machines it covers, there is no real reason to use late loading."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:220
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"It might be still possible to manually force late loading of microcode.  But"
" it may cause kernel malfunction and you should take the risk yourself.  You"
" will need to reconfigure your kernel for late loading, but early loading is"
" always supported by Linux kernel version 6.6 or later on a x86 (no matter "
"32-bit or 64-bit) system.  The instructions here will show you how to create"
" an initrd for early loading. It is also possible to build the same "
"microcode bin file into the kernel, which allows early loading but requires "
"the kernel to be recompiled to update the microcode."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:232
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"To confirm what processor(s) you have (if more than one, they will be "
"identical) look in /proc/cpuinfo. Determine the decimal values of the cpu "
"family, model and stepping by running the following command (it will also "
"report the current microcode version):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:238
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid "<userinput>head -n7 /proc/cpuinfo</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:241
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Convert the cpu family, model and stepping to pairs of hexadecimal digits, "
"and remember the value of the <quote>microcode</quote> field.  You can now "
"check if there is any microcode available."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:247
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"If you are creating an initrd to update firmware for different machines, as "
"a distro would do, go down to 'Early loading of microcode' and cat all the "
"Intel blobs to GenuineIntel.bin or cat all the AMD blobs to "
"AuthenticAMD.bin. This creates a larger initrd - for all Intel machines in "
"the 20200609 update the size was 3.0 MB compared to typically 24 KB for one "
"machine."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><title>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:256
#, xml-text
msgid "Intel Microcode for the CPU"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:259
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"The first step is to get the most recent version of the Intel microcode.  "
"This must be done by navigating to <ulink url= "
"'https://github.com/intel/Intel-Linux-Processor-Microcode-Data-"
"Files/releases/'/> and downloading the latest file there.  As of this "
"writing the most secure version of the microcode is microcode-&intel-"
"microcode-version;.  Extract this file in the normal way, the microcode is "
"in the <filename>intel-ucode </filename> directory, containing various blobs"
" with names in the form XX-YY-ZZ. There are also various other files, and a "
"release note."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:271
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"In the past, intel did not provide any details of which blobs had changed "
"versions, but now the release note details this.  You can compare the "
"microcode version in <filename>/proc/cpuinfo</filename> with the version for"
" your CPU model in the releasenote to know if there is an update."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:279
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"The recent firmware for older processors is provided to deal with "
"vulnerabilities which have now been made public, and for some of these such "
"as Microarchitectural Data Sampling (MDS) you might wish to increase the "
"protection by disabling hyperthreading, or alternatively to disable the "
"kernel's default mitigation because of its impact on compile times. Please "
"read the online documentation at <ulink url= "
"'https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/hw-vuln/index.html'/>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:289
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"For a Meteor Lake mobile (described as Intel(R) Core(TM) Ultra 5 125H CPU) "
"the relevant values are cpu family 6, model 140, stepping 4 so in this case "
"the required identification is 06-aa-04. The releasenote says the latest "
"microcode for it is versioned 0x25.  If the value of the "
"<quote>microcode</quote> field in <filename>/proc/cpuinfo</filename> is 0x25"
" or greater, it indicates the microcode update is already applied by the "
"BIOS.  Otherwise, proceed to <xref linkend='early-microcode'/>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><title>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:301
#, xml-text
msgid "AMD Microcode for the CPU"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:304
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"Begin by downloading a container of firmware for your CPU family from <ulink"
" url= '&sources-anduin-http;/linux-firmware/amd-ucode/'/>.  The family is "
"always specified in hex. Families 10h to 14h (16 to 20)  are in "
"microcode_amd.bin.  Families 15h, 16h, 17h (Zen, Zen+, Zen2) and 19h (Zen3) "
"have their own containers, but very few machines are likely to get updated "
"microcode. Instead, AMD provide an updated AGESA to the motherboard makers, "
"who may provide an updated BIOS using this.  There is a Python3 script at "
"<ulink url= "
"'https://github.com/AMDESE/amd_ucode_info/blob/master/amd_ucode_info.py'/>."
"  Download that script and run it against the bin file to check which "
"processors have updates."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:319
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"For the very old Athlon(tm) II X2 in these examples the values were cpu "
"family 16, model 5, stepping 3 giving an identification of Family=0x10 "
"Model=0x05 Stepping=0x03.  One line of the "
"<command>amd_ucode_info.py</command> script output describes the microcode "
"version for it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:326
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid ""
"<computeroutput>Family=0x10 Model=0x05 Stepping=0x03: Patch=0x010000c8 "
"Length=960 bytes</computeroutput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:329
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"If the value of the <quote>microcode</quote> field in "
"<filename>/proc/cpuinfo</filename> is 0x10000c8 or greater, it indicates the"
" BIOS has already applied the microcode update.  Otherwise, proceed to <xref"
" linkend='early-microcode'/>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><title>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:337
#, xml-text
msgid "Early loading of microcode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:340
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"If you have established that updated microcode is available for your system,"
" it is time to prepare it for early loading. This requires an additional "
"package, <xref linkend='cpio'/> and the creation of an initrd which will "
"need to be added to grub.cfg."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:347
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"It does not matter where you prepare the initrd, and once it is working you "
"can apply the same initrd to later LFS systems or newer kernels on this same"
" machine, at least until any newer microcode is released. Use the following "
"commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:353
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid ""
"<userinput>mkdir -p initrd/kernel/x86/microcode\n"
"cd initrd</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:357
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"For an AMD machine, use the following command (replace &lt;MYCONTAINER&gt; "
"with the name of the container for your CPU's family):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:362
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid ""
"<userinput>cp -v ../&lt;MYCONTAINER&gt; "
"kernel/x86/microcode/AuthenticAMD.bin</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:365
#, xml-text
msgid "Or for an Intel machine copy the appropriate blob using this command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:368
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid ""
"<userinput>cp -v ../intel-ucode/&lt;XX-YY-ZZ&gt; "
"kernel/x86/microcode/GenuineIntel.bin</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:394
#, xml-text
msgid "Now prepare the initrd:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:397
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid ""
"<userinput>find . | cpio -o -H newc &gt; /boot/microcode.img</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:400
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"You now need to add a new entry to /boot/grub/grub.cfg and here you should "
"add a new line after the linux line within the stanza.  If /boot is a "
"separate mountpoint:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:405
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid "<userinput>initrd /microcode.img</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:408
#, xml-text
msgid "or this if it is not:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:411
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid "<userinput>initrd /boot/microcode.img</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:414
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"If you are already booting with an initrd (see <xref "
"linkend=\"initramfs\"/>), you should run <command>mkinitramfs</command> "
"again after putting the appropriate blob or container into <filename "
"class=\"directory\">/usr/lib/firmware</filename>. More precisely, put an "
"intel blob in a <filename class=\"directory\">/usr/lib/firmware/intel-"
"ucode</filename> directory or an AMD container in a <filename "
"class=\"directory\">/usr/lib/firmware/amd-ucode</filename> directory before "
"running <command>mkinitramfs</command>.  Alternatively, you can have both "
"initrd on the same line, such as <userinput>initrd /microcode.img /other-"
"initrd.img</userinput> (adapt that as above if /boot is not a separate "
"mountpoint)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:429
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"You can now reboot with the added initrd, and then use the following command"
" to check that the early load worked:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:433
#, no-wrap, xml-text
msgid ""
"<userinput>dmesg | grep -e 'microcode' -e 'Linux version' -e 'Command "
"line'</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:436
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"If you updated to address vulnerabilities, you can look at the output of the"
" <command>lscpu</command> command to see what is now reported."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><para>
#: blfs-en/blfs-git/postlfs/config/firmware.xml:442
#, xml-text
msgid ""
"The places and times where early loading happens are very different in AMD "
"and Intel machines. First, an example of an Intel (Meteor Lake mobile) with "
"early loading:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <sect1><sect2><sect3><screen>
